  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\n500mb troughing will be in place across eastern Canada and New \nEngland through Thursday ahead of a cold front that will cross \nThursday afternoon and evening. For the rest of today into tonight, \nmultiple weak waves/vort. maxes will cross overhead, bringing \nchances for showers as well as a few storms. The airmass is pretty \ndry this afternoon, so coverage should remain fairly isolated and \nmostly in NH, but showers/storms will be capable of producing gusty \nwinds given how dry the low levels are (and possibly small hail\nwith cooling temperatures aloft). Chances for showers (30-50%) \ncontinue into this evening and through at least part of the \novernight hours as additional upper-level energy arrives. Lows \nwill be mostly in the 50s, and valley fog is possible in the \nnormally prone areas.\n\nA cold front approaches and crosses during the day Thursday, and \nwith better forcing, showers are possible in the morning and\nwill be followed by higher coverage of showers and \nthunderstorms in the afternoon to early evening hours. Ensemble\nmean of SBCAPE from the 12Z HREF depicts 500-1000 J/kg and \n0-6km shear around 30-35 kt to work with, so a couple of strong \nto marginally severe storms will be possible. However, \nconfidence is fairly low on where the better chances of seeing \nstrong/severe storm will be as CAMs are showing poor run to run \ncontinuity. Aside from showers/storms, temperatures will be \nclose to normal, which is mainly in the upper 70s to low 80s. \n\nThe front moves offshore Thursday evening, and aside from a couple \nof lingering upslope showers in the mountains, dry conditions are \nexpected overnight as a push of drier air arrives from the north. \nWinds behind the front will be pretty light, so patchy fog is a \npossibility, especially in valleys and in areas that receive rain. \nOvernight lows will be mostly in the 50s, except upper 40s possible \nin the north.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nBy Friday morning, low pressure departs to the northeast. A\nlight pressure gradient in it's wake could allow for some light\nnorthwesterly winds through the day on Friday. Some wraparound\nmoisture from this low may allow for the development of an\nisolated shower or two over central Maine Friday afternoon.\n\nOn Saturday, high pressure moves in from Canada and remains\noverhead through most of the weekend. This will allow for mostly\nclear skies and calm winds on Saturday and through most of the\nday Sunday. Near average mid-level heights will translate to\nnear-average temperatures. High temperatures are forecast to be\naround 80F in most of the population corridor, though cooler\nhighs in the lower 70s are expected north of the mountains.\n\nOn Sunday a mid-level ridge moves eastward, and a frontal\npassage may cross the northeast Sunday evening. However, it\nshould be pretty late in the evening before the front makes it\ninto NH. The front looks to pass over the region Sunday night\nand into Monday morning. High pressure is likely to follow the\nfront, with drier and clearer skies in the forecast through much\nof the remainder of next week.",
